    display: []
    titleName: 'Introduction

'
    description: 'Whenever you have a problem with your computer, don''t panic! There
      are many basic troubleshooting techniques you can use to fix issues like this.
      In this module, we''ll show you some simple things to try when troubleshooting,
      as well as how to solve common problems you may encounter.

      

      Troubleshooting
      is a step-by-step process used to determine the source of a problem in a computer
      system and fix any hardware or software issues. It is important to approach
      problem-solving in a logical and organized manner in order to effectively resolve
      the issue. While having experience is helpful in problem-solving, following
      a troubleshooting model can improve efficiency and speed in finding a solution.

      

      One
      basic troubleshooting method is to press Control-Alt-Delete on a PC, which
      opens the Microsoft Windows Task Manager where users can determine which applications
      have become unresponsive, close them and restart the computer.

'
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display: []
    titleName: How to Troubleshoot Common Problems in a Computer
    description: "A. Power \u2013 Power button does not start the computer\r\n1.Check
      the power strip or surge protector to make sure it is plugged in and turned
      on. You may have to reset the surge protector by turning it off and then back
      on. You may also plug other device to verify if it is working properly.\r\n2.Check
      for loose cables in the back of the computer (especially the main power cable).\r\n3.Check
      the outlet for power problems or try moving the power cable to a different
      outlet.\r\n4.Try a different power cable.\r\n5.If you are using a laptop, the
      battery may not be charged. Plug the AC adapter into the outlet, then try to
      turn on the laptop. If it still doesn't start up, you may need to wait a few
      minutes and try again.\r\n6.Discharge the computer:\r\n7.Unplug all cables
      from the back of the computer (note the positions for all the cables) including
      power.\r\n8.Leave the computer unplugged with no power to the unit for a minimum
      of 15 minutes to allow the computer to discharge.\r\n9.After discharging the
      computer, plug the power cable back in and check for a status light on the
      power supply. If the status light on the power supply still does not illuminate,
      the power supply may need to be replaced.\r\n\r\nB. Display/Video \u2013 your
      monitor may be experiencing an LCD display or video issue usually caused by
      either the monitor, video card (GPU) or video settings. Some symptoms that
      indicate LCD display or video issue are blank or black screen, color fade,
      fuzzy, blurry, distorted or stretched image, light leakage or bleeding, flickering,
      horizontal or vertical lines, light and dark patches, dead pixel or bright
      pixel.\r\n\r\n1.Verify display or video issue on a known-good monitor\r\na.
      A basic way to identify this is to connect the PC to a known-good external
      monitor or TV and ensure that the display (S-video, VGA, DVI, HDMI or DisplayPort)
      cables are firmly connected to the video ports on the PC and the monitor.\r\nb.
      If the issue persists on the monitor, it may be due to the video card or video
      settings and not the monitor itself.\r\n2. Check for physical Damages\r\na.
      Performance issues can arise if the display cables or LCD screen are damaged.
      Symptoms may include the screen not working, intermittent functioning, color
      mismatches, flickering, or horizontal/vertical lines on the display.\r\n3.
      Verify display or video issue in Windows Safe Mode\r\na. Windows Safe Mode
      allows us to identify if the issue is related to the operating system, video
      settings, device drivers or a 3rd party software.\r\n4. Update the video card
      (GPU) driver, monitor, chipset driver & BIOS\r\na. It is essential to update
      the following device drivers for optimal video performance and to resolve common
      video issues\r\n5. Download and install Microsoft Updates\r\na. Windows updates
      can support your Windows operating system in many ways. Windows updates can
      solve specific problems, provide protection from malicious attacks, or even
      add new features to the operating system.\r\n6. Change the video or display
      settings and adjust the brightness\r\nDisplay settings like brightness, refresh
      rate, resolution and power management may affect the performance of your monitor.
      Changing the display settings can help resolve several types of video issues.\r\n\r\nC.
      Audio \u2013 Fix no sound and other audio issues on you PC or laptop\r\n1.
      Check all physical connections, including plugs and jacks. Test any external
      volume control knobs. Disconnect and reconnect Bluetooth devices.\r\n2. Check
      the audio player controls. Many audio and video players will have their own
      separate audio controls. Make sure the sound is turned on and that the volume
      is turned up in the player.\r\n3. Run the audio troubleshooter to identify,
      diagnose, and fix problems.\r\n4. Verify that all Windows Updates are installed.
      Update the sound driver if necessary.\r\n\r\nD. Storage Device \u2013 also
      known as your hard disk or Solid-State Drive (SSD) for new versions. Storage
      device problems are often related to loose or incorrect cable connections,
      incorrect drive and media formats, and incorrect jumper and BIOS settings.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nCommon
      Problems and Solutions for Storage Devices\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nE.
      Cooling System \u2013 Common problem is noisy computer\r\n1.Clean out the inside
      of the computer. The main cause of a noisy computer is usually dust buildup
      inside. Cleaning out your computer will help keep the heat down and the noise
      to a minimum. Clean the inside of the computer every six months. Clean more
      frequently if you have pets or smoke.\r\n2. Replace your case fans. The fans
      inside your computer keep it cool, but are also the largest source of noise.
      Replacing your fans with larger, more-efficient ones can keep it quieter and
      cooler.The fans inside your computer keep it cool, but are also the largest
      source of noise. Replacing your fans with larger, more-efficient ones can keep
      it quieter and cooler.\r\n3. Replace your CPU cooler. One of the loudest fans
      in the computer is usually the CPU fan. This is because the CPU is typically
      the hottest component, so the CPU\r\nfan is often running at maximum speed.
      Replacing your CPU fan with a more efficient one can reduce the noise coming
      from your computer.\r\n\r\nF. Ports/Connectors \u2013 having problems with
      ports/connectors such as USB, VGA, Mouse and Keyboard ports can be resolved
      thru different steps.\r\n\r\nBefore troubleshooting any ports/connectors, make
      sure that the device drivers are already installed. For USB devices such as
      printers, and scanner are using specific application that needs to be installed
      to the computer. Follow the installation instructions from the USB Device manufacturer.\r\nSome
      troubleshooting steps require you to restart your PC. Before you begin troubleshooting,
      ensure to bookmark open web pages, save any open documents, spreadsheets, or
      any other unsaved work.\r\nUSB devices must be turned off or ejected before
      they are disconnected from the PC. Most USB devices support Windows Safely
      Remove Hardware feature.\r\n\r\nCheck USB ports or cables for physical damage\r\nUSB
      performance issues may occur if there is any type of damage caused to the USB
      cable or the USB ports on the PC. You may experience symptoms like a USB device
      stops working, works intermittently or the device wiggles when you plug it
      in etc.\r\nUSB cables and ports are exposed to elements that may cause a build-up
      of dust or even physical damages. If you notice that the port or connector
      has dust or other contaminants, use a can of compressed air to clean the ports
      and connectors.\r\nWarning: Do not use any type of cleaning solution, water
      or even a damp cloth. Disconnect the USB device and the cable before using
      the can of compressed air to clean the ports and connectors.\r\n\r\nCheck Windows
      Device Manager for errors\r\nWindows Device Manager can help you identify and
      troubleshoot any device driver related issues with the USB device.\r\nTo view
      the USB devices in Windows Device Manager:\r\nPress the Windows logo + R key
      on the keyboard.\r\nType devmgmt.msc in the run dialog box and press the Enter
      key.\r\nExpand the Universal Serial Bus controllers section.\r\n\r\nG. Drivers
      \u2013 sometimes, symptoms may appear such as hardware not working properly
      may lead to problems with the drivers.\r\n\r\nCheck some solutions to resolve
      the problem.\r\n1.Check Device Manager - Open Device Manager by right-clicking
      on the Start menu and selecting \"Device Manager\". Look for devices with a
      yellow triangle or red cross. These indicate problems with the drivers.\r\n2.
      Update Drivers - Right-click on the problematic device in Device Manager. Select
      \"Update driver\" and choose \"Search automatically for updated driver software\".
      If Windows finds a new driver, it will install it automatically.\r\n3. Roll
      Back Drivers: Sometimes new driver updates can cause issues. If a device starts
      malfunctioning after a driver update, you can roll back to the previous driver.
      Right-click on the device in Device Manager, select \"Properties\", go to the
      \"Driver\" tab, and click \"Roll Back Driver\".\r\n4. Reinstall Drivers: Uninstall
      the problematic driver by right-clicking on the device in Device Manager and
      selecting \"Uninstall device\". Restart your computer. Windows will attempt
      to reinstall the driver automatically. If Windows does not reinstall the driver,
      you can download it from the manufacturer's website and install it manually.\r\n5.
      Check for Operating System Updates: Sometimes, updating your operating system
      can fix driver issues as updates can include new drivers and patches.\r\nGo
      to Settings > Update & Security > Windows Update and check for updates.\r\n6.
      Download Drivers from Manufacturer\u2019s Website: Visit the hardware manufacturer's
      website and download the latest drivers for your specific hardware model. Install
      the downloaded driver and restart your computer if necessary.\r\n7. Check Compatibility:
      Ensure that the driver you are trying to install is compatible with your operating
      system and hardware. Sometimes, incompatibility can cause driver issues.\r\n8.
      Scan for Malware: Malware can corrupt or replace legitimate drivers with malicious
      ones. Run a full system scan using reputable antivirus software to check for
      malware infections.\r\n9. Seek Professional Help: If you are unable to resolve
      the issue, consider seeking help from a professional technician or the device
      manufacturer\u2019s support team.\r\n"
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display: []
    titleName: 'Steps of Troubleshooting

'
    description: 'Step 1: Define the Problem

      The first step is the most important.
      You need to gather information about what unexpected things happened before
      the problem occurred and what related symptoms and unique circumstances occurred
      before the incident that led to the problem.

      

      These lists of
      questions can serve as your guide.

      1.Was the computer working properly
      before this problem occurred?

      2.When did you first notice the problem
      or error?

      3.Have you made any changes to the software and hardware?

      4.Has
      anything happened to the computer? Was it dropped or was something dropped
      on it? Was coffee or soda spilled on the keyboard?

      5.When exactly does
      the problem or error occur? Is it during the startup process? After lunch?
      Only on Monday morning? After using e-mail?

      6.What does the problem
      or error look like?

      7.Were there any changes in the computer coinciding
      with the problem such as noise, screen changes, lights, and so forth?

      8.Are
      there any error message?

      9.Were there any funny noises or smells present
      at the time the computer failed?

      10.Are there current backups of the
      system and data files available?

      If one question leads to another question,
      determine if the question is important. If so, ask a follow-up question. If
      not, file it for later review.

      At this point examine the computer.

      

      

      Step
      2: Isolate the Problem/ Types of Errors

      Sometimes a problem can have
      many possible causes. A trial-and-error method is used to eliminate various
      options. The best approach is to look for the most straightforward cause first,
      even when working with a complex system.

      One common testing method
      is the split-half troubleshooting approach. This technique isolates the source
      of a problem through a process of elimination. This method works best when
      the system involved has a number of parts in series. Troubleshooters first
      test halfway down the line of components. If the middle component works, they
      know everything before it works. At this point, they move on to the middle
      of the remaining untested section toward the end of the series. If the test
      of this second section works, they go on to the midpoint of the remaining section.

      If
      the problem occurs at any point in this testing, then the troubleshooters do
      a split back toward the start of the series until the problem part is found.
      The split-half process can save time in systems that depend on many components.

      

      Step
      3: Conduct the Repair

      After you isolate the problem and determine the
      possible source of the problem on a few categories the process of elimination
      begins.

      

      Make a Diagnostic Plan

      Create a diagnostic
      plan to further troubleshoot the problem based on your knowledge and experience.
      Your plan should start with the most obvious and/or the easiest solution to
      eliminate the possible source of the problem and to move to another.

      

      Diagnostic
      plan is a simple checklist of what needs to be done based on symptoms and that
      also includes the tools, materials and equipment necessary for the diagnostic.

      

      Follow
      the plan from beginning to End

      Once you have the plan, it is important
      to follow it completely. Unsystematically trying things can result to a more
      serious problem. Document every action you take as well as its results. If
      the initial plan is unsuccessful, then create another one based on the result
      of the previous plan.

      

      

      

      

      After pinpointing
      the source of the problem, you may either repair or replace the defective component.
      Again, whether the problem is hardware or software, be sure to record your
      solution.

      

      

      Step 4: Confirm the Results

      To
      finish the repair, follow these steps to ensure the job is done right:

      1.Verify
      the problem is fixed. Ask the user to test the computer and confirm their requirements
      are met.

      2.Ensure the fix didn''t cause new problems. If you found
      or caused any other issues during troubleshooting, make sure to fix those too.

      

      Step
      5: Document the Results

      Write down the problem and the steps taken
      to fix it. Keeping this information will be helpful if the same issue happens
      again. You can share these notes with other technicians to learn from each
      other. Also, this record will be useful if you have questions about the work
      that was done.

'
    has3dView: 0
